course: space
lesson: 29
title: The history of space exploration
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: For thousands of years people could only look up; in the last 80 years we have sent machines, animals and people into space.
explain: The space age began in a race between the Soviet Union and the USA: the Soviets launched the first satellite and the first person, and the Americans first landed people on the Moon.
explain: Space history has dark pages too: the first rocket to reach space was a wartime weapon built partly by prisoners, the dog Laika never came home, and two Shuttle crews died.
explain: Think of the night your family watched something live on TV together: in July 1969 hundreds of millions of people watched Neil Armstrong step onto the Moon.
explain: Today countries work together: the International Space Station has had people living on board without a break since 2000.
fact: The first rocket to reach space was a German V-2 in 1944, a weapon built partly by forced labourers and prisoners, thousands of whom died.
fact: On 4 October 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite (a machine people put in orbit round Earth), a metal ball that beeped radio signals as it orbited.
fact: In November 1957 a dog called Laika became the first animal to orbit Earth, but she died within hours because there was no way to bring her back.
fact: Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space on 12 April 1961, and Valentina Tereshkova the first woman in 1963, both from the Soviet Union.
fact: On 20 July 1969 Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon during Apollo 11, while Michael Collins orbited above.
fact: Between 1969 and 1972 twelve astronauts, all American men, walked on the Moon, and no one has walked there since.
fact: Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, launched in 1977, flew past the giant planets, and Voyager 2 is still the only spacecraft to visit Uranus and Neptune.
fact: NASA's Space Shuttles flew from 1981 to 2011, but two were lost with their crews, Challenger in 1986 and Columbia in 2003; after 2011 Russian Soyuz capsules, and later SpaceX capsules, kept taking crews to the space station.
fact: The International Space Station has had people living on board without a break since November 2000, built by the USA, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada together.
fact: Helen Sharman became the first Briton in space in 1991, and Tim Peake the first British ESA astronaut to visit the space station in 2015-2016.
fact: Andy Thomas flew in 1996 as the first Australian-born NASA astronaut.
fact: NASA has landed rovers on Mars since 1997, and Perseverance, landing in 2021, brought the helicopter Ingenuity, which made the first powered flights on another planet.
fact: In December 2015 a SpaceX Falcon 9 booster landed upright back on Earth after a launch to orbit, and reused boosters are now routine.
exercise: What was the first rocket to reach space? | A German V-2 in 1944 | Apollo 11 in 1969 | Sputnik 1 in 1957 | A Space Shuttle in 1981
exercise: Who built the V-2 rockets, in part? | Forced labourers and prisoners | Volunteer astronauts who wanted to fly | NASA engineers | Schoolchildren in a contest
exercise: What was Sputnik 1? | The first artificial satellite | The first person in space | The first Moon rover | The first space station
exercise: When was Sputnik 1 launched? | On 4 October 1957 | On 12 April 1961 | On 20 July 1969 | In November 2000
exercise: Who was the first animal to orbit Earth? | The dog Laika | A chimpanzee named Ham | A cat named Félicette | A monkey named Albert
exercise: What happened to Laika? | She died within hours, as there was no way back | She landed safely in a field and lived to an old age | She was rescued from orbit by a team of astronauts | She stayed on a space station and was looked after there
exercise: Who was the first person in space? | Yuri Gagarin | Neil Armstrong | Valentina Tereshkova | Buzz Aldrin
exercise: Who was the first woman in space? | Valentina Tereshkova | Helen Sharman | Sally Ride | Laika
exercise: Who stayed in orbit while Armstrong and Aldrin walked on the Moon? | Michael Collins | Yuri Gagarin | Tim Peake | Andy Thomas
exercise: How many people have walked on the Moon? | Twelve | Two | Twenty-four | None
exercise: Which spacecraft is the only one to have visited Uranus and Neptune? | Voyager 2 | Voyager 1 | Sputnik 1 | Perseverance
exercise: Which two Space Shuttles were lost with their crews? | Challenger and Columbia | Discovery and Endeavour | Atlantis and Apollo | Sputnik and Voyager
exercise: Since when have people lived on the space station without a break? | November 2000 | July 1969 | October 1957 | April 2021
exercise: Who was the first Briton in space? | Helen Sharman | Tim Peake | Andy Thomas | Yuri Gagarin
exercise: What did the helicopter Ingenuity do? | Made the first powered flights on another planet | Flew the first astronauts all the way to the Moon | Landed on Neptune | Carried Sputnik into orbit
exercise: What did a SpaceX Falcon 9 booster first do in December 2015? | Land upright back on Earth after a launch to orbit | Carry the first people all the way to Mars and back | Reach the Moon | Fly past Uranus
test: Jayden says "Americans were the first people in space". What should you tell him? | No, Yuri Gagarin of the Soviet Union was first, in 1961 | Yes, Neil Armstrong was the first, in 1961, before Gagarin | Yes, the Shuttle went first in 1957 | No, a Briton was first, in 1991
test: grandad reckons people go to the Moon all the time now. true? | No, nobody has walked there since 1972 | Yes, astronauts visit it every year or so | Yes, there is a Moon base there now with people living in it | No, nobody has ever gone further than the space station
test: Which is true about the first artificial satellite? | It was a beeping metal ball launched by the Soviet Union | It was a US rocket that carried a small dog round the Earth | It was the International Space Station | It was a German V-2 weapon
test: Why is the V-2 a sad part of space history? | It was a weapon built partly by prisoners, thousands of whom died | It never reached space | It carried the very first astronauts up into space and then crashed | It was built only by children
test: A cartoon shows Laika coming home a hero and wagging her tail. What is wrong? | Laika died in space within hours; there was no way back | Laika was really a cat called Félicette, not a dog at all | Laika never went into space | Laika lived on the Moon
test: Who were the first people to land on the Moon? | Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin | Neil Armstrong and Michael Collins | Yuri Gagarin and Valentina Tereshkova | Helen Sharman and Tim Peake
test: Why did the end of the Shuttle flights in 2011 matter less for the space station? | Other spacecraft kept taking crews there | The station was closed down in 2011 as well | The Shuttle was never used to build or visit it | Nobody was living on the station at that time
test: If you met Tim Peake, what could you ask him about? | His stay on the space station in 2015-2016 | His walk on the Moon with Neil Armstrong in 1969 | His flight past Neptune | His time on Mars
test: Which countries built the International Space Station together? | The USA, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada | The USA alone | Russia and China | Britain and Australia
test: my mate says the first flight on another planet was a plane on the Moon lol | No, it was the helicopter Ingenuity on Mars | Yes, a small plane flew on the Moon back in 1969 | No, it was a balloon on Venus | No, it was a drone on Jupiter
test: Why are reusable rocket boosters a big deal? | Landing and reusing them saves building new ones | They make rockets fast enough to fly to other stars | They make the astronauts lighter so they can float | They let rockets fly up without needing any fuel at all
exam: Identify the person who became the first human being to travel into space, on 12 April 1961. | Yuri Gagarin | Neil Armstrong | Valentina Tereshkova | Michael Collins
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the satellite Sputnik 1? | It was the first artificial satellite, launched by the Soviet Union in 1957 | It was the first crewed spacecraft, launched by the USA in 1961 | It was the first space station, occupied since 2000 | It was the first rover to land on Mars
exam: Ascertain the number of persons who walked upon the surface of the Moon between 1969 and 1972. | Twelve | Two | Six | Twenty
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the Space Shuttle programme. | It operated from 1981 to 2011, and two Shuttles were lost with their crews | It operated from 1957 to 1969, during which period no Shuttle was ever lost | It carried the first astronauts to the Moon | It continues to operate at the present time
